// Practice quiz: aggregate functions
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters and treats colon, equals, hash, tilde and braces as syntax, so those are escaped.

$CATEGORY: sql-jdbc-foundations/crud-aggregates-and-joins/aggregate-functions

::o1 largest value::Which aggregate returns the single highest order total in an orders table? {
=MAX(total)#Correct. MAX returns the largest non-NULL value of its input.
~COUNT(total)#Not quite. COUNT returns how many totals are known, not how large they are.
~SUM(total)#Not quite. SUM adds every total together.
~AVG(total)#Not quite. AVG returns the mean, not the highest value.
}

::o1 average with a NULL::A column holds 10, 20 and NULL. What does AVG on that column return? {
~10#Not quite. That would treat NULL as 0 and divide by three.
~NULL#Not quite. One NULL input does not make the whole average NULL.
=15#Correct. AVG ignores NULL inputs, so it averages 10 and 20.
~30#Not quite. That is the sum, not the average.
}

::o2 star versus column::A members table has 5 rows, and email is NULL in 2 of them. What do COUNT(*) and COUNT(email) return? {
~5 and 5#Not quite. COUNT(email) skips the two NULL emails.
~3 and 3#Not quite. COUNT(*) counts every row, whatever its columns hold.
~3 and 5#Not quite. The two meanings are reversed.
=5 and 3#Correct. COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(email) counts only the rows where email is not NULL.
}

::o2 no input rows::A WHERE clause matches no rows at all. What do COUNT(*) and SUM(amount) return? {
~0 and 0#Not quite. SUM of no rows is NULL, not zero; wrap it in COALESCE if a report needs 0.
=0 and NULL#Correct. COUNT returns 0 for no rows, while the other aggregates return NULL when they have no input.
~NULL and NULL#Not quite. COUNT is the exception\: it returns 0.
~An ERROR for both#Not quite. Aggregates over zero rows are legal.
}
